<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Arolsen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arolsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Arolsen is a small town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From the 18th century it was the residence of the counts and princes of Waldeck, who built a large baroque palace there, and it remained the capital of the Free State of Waldeck until 1929. It is home to the Arolsen Archives, the international centre for documents on Nazi persecution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-arolsen.jpg" alt="Bad Arolsen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Innenstadt_Bad_Arolsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
